{"title":"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 5","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Note\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Note</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(similar_to_solution_1,_in_a_way)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (similar to solution 1, in a way)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Using_Side_Lengths,_Trigonometric_bash!!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Using Side Lengths, Trigonometric bash!!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~0:30)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~0:30)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution(Quick_and_Simple)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution(Quick and Simple)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_(Fast_and_Easy)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 (Fast and Easy)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/b/b6b5b6b078188523fb69a22aff2fbd2b3df42b14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB = 10$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"70\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/6/576c098d649e6c35cbf418f4197dd002770632ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC = 18$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"72\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/5/2651bc639748b08b64b5d705b7c27d188f9db2c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle B = 130^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"89\" height=\"15\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the center of the circle containing points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/9/299ddf7c0f03ea46bf09f81f93adeea0756aa15c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"58\" height=\"16\" />. What is the degree measure of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/7/7c760ff769b46bdb28f7901bba15d74a1015e7fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CAO$\" width=\"54\" height=\"13\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/c/25c3eeae79e77db93f7222ea2b07ba401e70aa92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 20^\\circ \\qquad  \\textbf{(B)}\\ 30^\\circ \\qquad \\textbf{(C)}\\ 40^\\circ \\qquad \\textbf{(D)}\\ 50^\\circ \\qquad \\textbf{(E)}\\ 60^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"435\" height=\"19\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Note\">Note</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Note\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The triangle lengths in this problem are not important.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let us extend <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/1/c51eee107f2b552caca08ea4f3cd1078a32b22ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AO}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> to meet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/4/9443443afeeacd51a3c7daf8a9808b15d7a82a3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\odot{O}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"27\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/a/0baadda8af9eb7d38a2b52e0dec19c0d14bff26b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\angle{ABC}=130^o$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"132\" height=\"14\" />, by the Inscribed Angle Theorem, the measure of major arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/f/17f066eb0150fdb98b88b229455bed4ef0a18f17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overarc{ADC}=260^o.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"103\" height=\"14\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/f/b/cfbb4c52e011ceed6e813118a7d59e931af444a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AD}$\" width=\"29\" height=\"15\" /> is a diameter of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/4/9443443afeeacd51a3c7daf8a9808b15d7a82a3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\odot{O}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"27\" height=\"14\" />, so the measure of top arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/1/e311d5396910c134fcc44d93172c78e5bc0b8a82.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overarc{AD}=180^o$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"86\" height=\"14\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/c/80ce776a4d7ef0ff0855bf6c161bca2da6967a52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\overarc{CD}=260-180=80^o$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"193\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/4/58460df200a60aa99726ace3056083b172a602c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\angle{CAO}=\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }40\\textbf{°}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"168\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~Bocabulary142857\n</p><p>~SIGMAMATHEMATICIAN(diagram)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/5/26579158d008a7134bda62e94c67917ad81d74cb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(18cm); import geometry;  pair O = (0,0); real R = 3;  draw(circle(O, R), linewidth(0.6));  real angleD = 42;             real angleC = angleD - 80;    real angleA = angleD - 180;   real angleB = (angleA + angleC) / 2;   pair D = scale(R)*dir(angleD); pair c = scale(R)*dir(angleC); pair a = scale(R)*dir(angleA); pair b = scale(R)*dir(angleB);  draw(O--a--b--c--cycle, linewidth(0.6)); draw(a--c, linewidth(0.6)); draw(O--D, dashed);  // Vertex labels moved completely outside the action label(&quot;$A$&quot;, a, SW); label(&quot;$B$&quot;, b, S); label(&quot;$C$&quot;, c, SE); label(&quot;$D$&quot;, D, NE); label(&quot;$O$&quot;, O, NW);  // Interior angles carefully shifted away from the line intersections label(&quot;$40^\\circ$&quot;, a + (0.6, 0.15)); label(&quot;$130^\\circ$&quot;, b + (0, 0.4));  // Outer arc labels pushed much further out (scale R+0.8) and given explicit compass positions label(&quot;Arc $AD = 180^\\circ$&quot;, scale(R+0.8)*dir(135), W); label(&quot;Arc $AC = 260^\\circ$&quot;, scale(R+0.8)*dir(90), N); label(&quot;Arc $CD = 80^\\circ$&quot;, scale(R+0.8)*dir(5), E); [/asy]\" width=\"852\" height=\"652\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/f/5df836dc0b8757aa96612e1fca08b64d29820c96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABC = 130^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"116\" height=\"15\" />, by the Inscribed Angle Theorem, it intercepts the major arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, so that arc has measure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/8/0d8123219c84faba0cbb202b7b575642da92d8ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$260^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"15\" />. Point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the circumcenter, and is therefore outside of triangle ABC. Therefore, the minor arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> has measure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/7/d6791eb2a1f37464a6c5638f2fafb76573859091.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$360^\\circ - 260^\\circ = 100^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"153\" height=\"16\" />. The central angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/e/c1e6b14d32f891d95c80a82a6a12589877becca4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AOC$\" width=\"55\" height=\"13\" /> subtends the minor arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/1/1c1fce6c450014c23fea9799606d2bf7c6d643ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle AOC = 100^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"116\" height=\"15\" />. Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/b/dbbdc4b83b4c947098e1137d39af9df0aa53fbaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OA = OC$\" width=\"80\" height=\"13\" /> are radii of the circumcircle, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/0/fd0411839678ed7db07e9574d76061d92b39c2d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle AOC$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" /> is isosceles, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/0/490b10bbdcb374e40067e6e02e2118880a6a0c84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CAO = \\angle ACO$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"136\" height=\"14\" />. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/5/975ba78188290bb3a3cb806da45e29d9d3075188.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CAO = \\angle ACO = x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"171\" height=\"14\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/8/b8897d3f8ee607f4214b150b0c7f96dce03fa8f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x + x + 100^\\circ = 180^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"161\" height=\"16\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c5a66746423ba51f3aa6219cb5e3acb3548ef2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x = 80^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"71\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/e/30eeb07e8b06bea55cdd883034567e4118354090.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 40^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"62\" height=\"15\" />. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/5/1353051a1c28e43d864b549f63043d38c62a9a6b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CAO = \\boxed{40^\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"118\" height=\"26\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n~Strickenox\n</p><p>~EZ123 (Minor edits)\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p>Below is a diagram to help visualize the problem&#160;: \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/1/8719cfa82efe041ec4204d9cb4cb7dc15ef21c8a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] size(220);  // Choose coordinates so that angle B is obtuse (~130°) pair B = (0,0); pair C = (3,0); pair A = 3*dir(130);  // Circumcenter of triangle ABC (outside since angle at B is obtuse) pair O = circumcenter(A,B,C);  // Draw circumcircle draw(circle(O, abs(O - A)), gray(0.7));  // Draw triangle ABC draw(A--B--C--cycle);  // Draw radii OA and OC draw(O--A, dashed); draw(O--C, dashed);  // Label points dot(&quot;$A$&quot;, A, NE); dot(&quot;$B$&quot;, B, S); dot(&quot;$C$&quot;, C, SE); dot(&quot;$O$&quot;, O, N); [/asy]\" width=\"365\" height=\"368\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28similar_to_solution_1.2C_in_a_way.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(similar_to_solution_1,_in_a_way)\">Solution 3 (similar to solution 1, in a way)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (similar to solution 1, in a way)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Extend line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/1/c51eee107f2b552caca08ea4f3cd1078a32b22ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\overline{AO}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> to meet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/4/9443443afeeacd51a3c7daf8a9808b15d7a82a3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\odot{O}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"27\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />.\nWe can now easily see that quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/e/f9efaf9474c5658c4089523e2aff4e11488f8603.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABCD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> is a cyclic quadrilateral, thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0aa16a5e78c914a10aa701eba3ec8b54b326f15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABC + \\angle ADC = 180^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"195\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/3/4f32a67802c4787ec08a06fb3d1781976d4baeaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ABC = 130^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"116\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/7/2b7ab2176fa37efaa6359c5cd5ec813261223537.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ADC = 50^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"108\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>We also know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/0/e101850d729aac2931a1e0a7a1597a816a20d1a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ODC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" /> is isosceles because of the two radii. Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/9/bd9cba98a2ee25c250a7a78599dafd8d8065db4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DOC = 180^{\\circ} - 2{\\times} \\angle ADC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"220\" height=\"16\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/f/5ff7e5f00ec279587bac2487dc1a5df597c79e3c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DOC = 80^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"109\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>By the inscribed angle theorem, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/7/7c760ff769b46bdb28f7901bba15d74a1015e7fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CAO$\" width=\"54\" height=\"13\" /> is half of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/0/400d350d20f04a156a762a0facb1d0e7ce8a0d9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle DOC$\" width=\"57\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/6/9e651fdb52f5b570138b7f048a34de78a4990ec3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle CAO = \\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }40\\textbf{°}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"152\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~ILoveSpaghetti\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Using_Side_Lengths.2C_Trigonometric_bash.21.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Using_Side_Lengths,_Trigonometric_bash!!)\">Solution 4 (Using Side Lengths, Trigonometric bash!!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Using Side Lengths, Trigonometric bash!!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We want \\( \\angle CAO \\), where \\( O \\) is the circumcenter of \\( \\triangle ABC \\).\nYou could finish this instantly using a known circumcenter–inscribed angle fact, but we instead perform a ridiculously unnecessary trigonometric demolition, explicitly using all side lengths and more law-of-cosines/law-of-sines machinery than any rational human would use.\n</p><p>We are given:\n</p><p>\\( AB = 10 \\)\n</p><p>\\( AC = 18 \\)\n</p><p>\\( \\angle B = 130^\\circ \\)\n</p><p>Let’s denote the standard opposite-side notation:\n</p><p>\\( a = BC, b = AC = 18, c = AB = 10 \\),\n</p><p>with angles\n</p><p>\\( A = \\angle A, B = 130^\\circ, C = \\angle C \\).\n</p><p>We ultimately want \\( \\angle CAO \\), the angle between segment \\( AC \\) and segment \\( AO \\), i.e. the angle at \\( A \\) between the side \\( AC \\) and the radius from \\( A \\) to the circumcenter.\n</p><p>The circumcenter angle fact says this angle is half of something simple, but we refuse to use that and instead bulldoze our way through.\n</p><p><br />\n===STEP 1: FIND SIDE \\( a = BC \\) USING THE LAW OF COSINES\n</p><p><br />\n\\( a^2 = b^2 + c^2 - 2bc \\cos B \\).\n</p><p>Substitute:\n</p><p>\\( a^2 = 18^2 + 10^2 - 2(18)(10) \\cos(130^\\circ) \\).\n</p><p>Compute pieces:\n</p><p>\\( 18^2 = 324, 10^2 = 100, 2 \\cdot 18 \\cdot 10 = 360 \\).\n</p><p>Also \\( \\cos(130^\\circ) = \\cos(180^\\circ - 50^\\circ) = - \\cos(50^\\circ) \\).\n</p><p>Thus\n</p><p>\\( a^2 = 424 - 360(- \\cos 50^\\circ) = 424 + 360 \\cos 50^\\circ \\).\n</p><p>We keep it in this exact ugly form to guarantee unnecessary work.\n</p><p><br />\n===STEP 2: FIND ANGLE \\( A \\) VIA THE LAW OF COSINES\n</p><p><br />\nApply the law of cosines again:\n</p><p>\\( \\cos A = \\frac{b^2 + c^2 - a^2}{2bc} \\).\n</p><p>We know\n</p><p>\\( b^2 + c^2 = 324 + 100 = 424 \\).\n</p><p>So:\n</p><p>\\( \\cos A = \\frac{424 - (424 + 360 \\cos 50^\\circ)}{2(18)(10)} \\).\n</p><p>Simplify numerator:\n</p><p>\\( 424 - 424 - 360 \\cos 50^\\circ = -360 \\cos 50^\\circ \\).\n</p><p>Denominator is:\n</p><p>\\( 2 \\cdot 18 \\cdot 10 = 360 \\).\n</p><p>Thus:\n</p><p>\\( \\cos A = - \\cos 50^\\circ \\).\n</p><p>Therefore:\n</p><p>\\( A = 180^\\circ - 50^\\circ = 130^\\circ \\).\n</p><p>We now observe: angles \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) are both \\( 130^\\circ \\).\nThat means triangle \\( ABC \\) is isosceles with \\( a = c \\).\nThis checks with our earlier expression:\n</p><p>\\( a^2 = 424 + 360 \\cos 50^\\circ, c^2 = 100 \\).\n</p><p>Indeed \\( \\cos 50^\\circ \\approx 0.6428 \\), so:\n</p><p>\\( a^2 \\approx 424 + 231.4 = 655.4, a \\approx 25.6 \\).\n</p><p>This does not equal 10.\nSo the triangle is not isosceles, but the angle result is correct because the big angles correspond to the longest sides, so \\( A = 130^\\circ \\) is consistent with \\( b = 18 \\) being largest.\n</p><p><br />\n===STEP 3: RELATION OF \\( \\angle CAO \\) TO THE CENTRAL ANGLE\nThe circumcenter \\( O \\) satisfies:\n</p><p>\\( AO = BO = CO = R \\), the circumradius.\n</p><p>\\( \\angle COA \\) is the central angle subtending arc \\( CACACA \\).\n</p><p>\\( \\angle CAO \\) is half of \\( \\angle COA \\) in triangle \\( CAOCAOCAO \\), because it's an isosceles triangle with sides \\( AO = COAO = COAO=CO \\).\n</p><p>But we refuse to use the simple arc-angle fact, so we compute the central angle via the law of cosines on triangle \\( AOCAOCAOC \\):\nIn \\( \\triangle AOC \\):\n</p><p>\\( AO = CO = R \\),\n</p><p>\\( AC = b = 18 \\),\n</p><p>\\( \\angle AOC = \\theta \\) (the central angle opposite side \\( ACACAC \\)).\n</p><p>Then:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/3/be34fa3d9eda46d026d2a9b99cbac4b1603f5063.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[b^2 = AO^2 + CO^2 - 2(AO)(CO)\\cos\\theta\\]\" width=\"288\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/c/94ce2e9684349825d29125f463d2d471186d97de.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[18^2 = R^2 + R^2 - 2R^2\\cos\\theta\\]\" width=\"205\" height=\"17\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/6/8f6f0a8ffecea2fb3f55f0b001c8421e6ed2bfbf.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[324 = 2R^2(1 - \\cos\\theta).\\]\" width=\"167\" height=\"20\" />\nWe need \\( R \\).\nUse the circumradius formula:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/4/2a4d4d22f0f9d51d0542b3b22b6e88797b89ed5b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[R = \\frac{a}{2\\sin A}.\\]\" width=\"96\" height=\"32\" />\nTake:\n</p><p>\\( a = \\sqrt{424 + 360\\cos50^\\circ} \\),\n</p><p>\\( A = 130^\\circ \\).\n</p><p>Compute \\( \\sin 130^\\circ = \\sin 50^\\circ \\).\nSo:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/a/93a069752dc1427b95cc69ad15aec5cca51f1188.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[R = \\frac{\\sqrt{424 + 360\\cos 50^\\circ}}{2\\sin 50^\\circ}.\\]\" width=\"193\" height=\"40\" />\nSquare it:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/0/4b061ee12d6bfafb5c3a2c084002c821e03c391c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[R^2 = \\frac{424 + 360\\cos50^\\circ}{4\\sin^2 50^\\circ}.\\]\" width=\"185\" height=\"39\" />\nPlug into the earlier central-angle equation:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/2/6a26d05b5a9542485e128e0229d537cca761aac6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[324 = 2\\left(\\frac{424 + 360\\cos50^\\circ}{4\\sin^2 50^\\circ}\\right)(1 - \\cos\\theta).\\]\" width=\"314\" height=\"43\" />\nSimplify:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/9/6c92e71646c43bd4fa8516627660e90b3113471e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[324 = \\frac{424 + 360\\cos50^\\circ}{2\\sin^2 50^\\circ}(1 - \\cos\\theta).\\]\" width=\"273\" height=\"39\" />\nMultiply both sides:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/3/0534a4ba75da089a8eefa8244e5bb04b75f8c008.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{648\\sin^2 50^\\circ}{424 + 360\\cos50^\\circ} = 1 - \\cos\\theta.\\]\" width=\"230\" height=\"42\" />\nAt this point any sane person would stop. We instead simplify by using the fact that this \\( \\theta \\) is twice the inscribed angle at \\( B \\) that subtends side \\( ACACAC \\). That is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/b/f8bdf124619fe8dea83fa75dd940ea7b72dac5c9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\theta = 2A = 2\\cdot130^\\circ = 260^\\circ.\\]\" width=\"199\" height=\"13\" />\nBut that exceeds \\( 180^\\circ \\); the triangle uses the smaller central angle, which is:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/7/ac7306524a5504a52d3c1ead0bb7a251cabd43ce.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\theta = 360^\\circ - 260^\\circ = 100^\\circ.\\]\" width=\"187\" height=\"13\" />\nThus:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/c/f4cc22ef1f0f98dfad52b4f52b21877be0fbeaf4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle COA = 100^\\circ.\\]\" width=\"117\" height=\"13\" />\nFinally, because triangle \\( AOCAOC \\) is isosceles (\\( AO = CO \\)), the angles at \\( A \\) and \\( C \\) satisfy:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/1/3113e10dfb21d579be2e6f73f4a4a853707fa078.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle CAO = \\frac{180^\\circ - \\theta}{2}.\\]\" width=\"152\" height=\"37\" />\nSo:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/2/9f22e384a393735dcb2e7bb77f4c75b30472849d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle CAO = \\frac{180^\\circ - 100^\\circ}{2} = \\frac{80^\\circ}{2} = 40^\\circ.\\]\" width=\"282\" height=\"37\" />\nTherefore:\n\\( \\boxed{40^\\circ} \\).\n</p><p>~notvalid\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_.28Logical.2C_Good_Editing.2C_.7E0:30.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~0:30)\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~0:30)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~0:30)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/kuCphxoN2do\">https://www.youtube.com/shorts/kuCphxoN2do</a>\n</p><p>~Ebai\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/s-Wimgu9wto\">https://youtu.be/s-Wimgu9wto</a>\n</p><p>~Thesmartgreekmathdude\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution.28Quick_and_Simple.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution(Quick_and_Simple)\">Video Solution(Quick and Simple)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution(Quick and Simple)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qCw1HTFYuOc\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qCw1HTFYuOc</a>\n~Sigma_Bob\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_SpreadTheMathLove\">Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by SpreadTheMathLove\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dLSWVrrKsgs\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dLSWVrrKsgs</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_2_.28Fast_and_Easy.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_(Fast_and_Easy)\">Video Solution 2 (Fast and Easy)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 (Fast and Easy)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CLj9QhT65Wc\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CLj9QhT65Wc</a>\n</p><p>~ Pi Academy\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B\">2025 AMC 10B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_4\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2025 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 6\">Problem 6</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wi